Regenerating desktop/src/demo/demo-incidents.json to carry incident.Link's
`clause` field - and the fold-aware replay tool that turned out to need,
built and verified in the process.

## 1. The gap this started from

desktop/src/demo/demo-incidents.json (docs/product/adr/0001-desktop-shell-
and-demo-mode.md: a real `netrewind incidents -o json` export from an
actual lab/inject.sh run) predates internal/incident.Link's `clause` field.
Every chain link's translated `why` text on the Incidents page, and on the
onboarding wizard's sample-investigation step, therefore fell back to
English - rulesCatalogue.ts's `whyFor` working exactly as designed against
a fixture it could not yet translate, not a code defect, but a real gap in
what demo mode shows. desktop/tests/visual/latin-text-scan.spec.ts (an
automated scan of the rendered Arabic app for untagged Latin-script
sentences) recorded the exact set of English sentences this produced as a
closed, tracked KNOWN_GAPS exception - not ignored, but pinned so a new or
different violation elsewhere still failed the suite.

lab/inject.sh needs Linux network namespaces and root, unavailable on the
machine doing this work, so the fixture could not simply be recaptured.
internal/correlate/replaydemo was written instead: read the unchanged
desktop/src/demo/demo-events.json (44 events), load rules/*.yaml through
the real correlate.LoadRules, create a real correlate.Engine, and replay
every event through engine.Offer in order - the same thing
cmd/netrewindd/writer.go's flush() does with a live batch, just without a
store.

## 2. The first run could not be trusted

    $ go run ./internal/correlate/replaydemo
    wrote 21 incidents (from 44 raw events) to desktop\src\demo\demo-incidents.json

16 incidents were expected; 21 were produced. The change was not
committed - the file was restored from the index immediately - and the
per-rule-id counts were compared between the two runs:

    rule                        original  naive replay
    link-down-isolated-hosts    2         1
    gateway-hijack               1         3
    contested-address            1         2
    reachability-lost            1         2
    default-route-moved          1         2
    default-route-lost           1         2
    service-unreachable          2         1
    change-broke-a-path          1         2
    rogue-dhcp-server            1         2
    port-flapping                1         0   (gone entirely)

Every other rule matched exactly. Two possibilities followed: a real
behaviour change in the engine or a rule since the fixture was captured, or
a flaw in how replaydemo replayed the recording. `port-flapping` dropping
to zero was the clearest thread to pull: its `flap` clause requires
`min_count: 3` on `link.down` (rules/port-flapping.yaml), and demo-
events.json has no group of 3 separate link.down entries for one
interface - only a single entry with a `count` field, which is what a
folded group looks like after `netrewind events -o json` has already
queried it.

## 3. Root cause

internal/store/sqlite.go's own fold SQL - `UPDATE events SET count = count
+ ?, ts_last = ? WHERE event_id = ?` - collapses a repeated fact within the
60s fold window into the *same* row rather than inserting a new one.
ts_wall (first occurrence) is never touched by folding; ts_last (latest
occurrence) is a store-internal column with no equivalent field on
event.Event at all, so nothing an export produces can say when the 2nd,
3rd, ... occurrence of a folded group actually happened.

cmd/netrewindd/writer.go's flush() calls store.Append on a raw batch
*first*, then loops the same raw batch through engine.Offer once per raw
event - folding happens inside Append, but every raw occurrence still gets
its own live Offer() call before it is ever folded away. A folded JSON
export is therefore not equivalent to what the engine actually saw:
replaying one Offer() call per folded entry silently drops (count-1) real
occurrences, which matters directly to any `min_count > 1` clause, and,
because the engine's window is shared and cumulative across every rule,
can shift what other rules conclude too. This is hypothesis (b), not (a):
a replay methodology defect, not an engine or rule regression.

## 4. The fix, in the four layers it was actually found in

Full detail and rationale live as doc comments on the functions themselves
(internal/correlate/replaydemo/main.go); this is the order they were found
and confirmed in, each one against the real, live-captured original file,
programmatically, not by matching incident totals alone.

1. **Unfold folded events.** `unfoldEvents` expands a folded event back
   into `count` separate copies before replay, restoring one Offer() call
   per raw occurrence.
2. **Upsert incidents by id.** `engine.Offer` can legitimately return more
   than one `*incident.Incident` for the same id as a later event
   completes an optional clause a thinner earlier match left unmet -
   engine.go's own dedup keeps the identity and replaces the thinner
   account. A naive append-only collection produced duplicate incident_ids
   with different content; `upsertIncident` replicates internal/store/
   incident_sqlite.go's own `ON CONFLICT(incident_id) DO UPDATE` exactly,
   including which fields it deliberately leaves alone (opened_at, title,
   rule_id, advice - fixed at first report).
3. **Evidence-based timestamp reconstruction.** Folding destroys the real
   spacing between a group's raw occurrences. A uniform guess does not
   work - spacings from 0s to the store's own 60s fold window were swept
   directly against the real original file, and every value left at least
   one rule wrong, because different folded groups had different real
   internal spacings, which folding is specifically designed to discard.
   What survives instead: the fixture this tool is about to overwrite
   already has, in its own chain data, exactly which occurrence (`rank`,
   from a clause's own `matched_count` evidence) closed at which real
   timestamp, or at minimum that *some* real timestamp was cited at all
   (`Link.At`, unranked). `loadKnownTimestamps` recovers both kinds from
   the file on disk before it is overwritten; `unfoldEvents` forward-fills
   ranked values into their exact position and assigns unranked ones to
   the highest slot that cannot displace a ranked one, and filters out a
   recovered value that merely restates ts_wall (every clause citing a
   folded group's lead event records that same baseline, which is not a
   second occurrence).
4. **Cap unfolding to what is actually needed.** rules/reachability-lost.
   yaml's optional, unrestricted `recovery` clause greedily matched a
   synthetic second copy of its own folded `loss` anchor instead of the
   real, distinct event the original incident used, once that anchor was
   unfolded to its full historical count regardless of whether anything
   needed the extra copy. A copy invented beyond what evidence or a rule's
   own `min_count` genuinely requires has nowhere principled to sit and is
   free to be picked up by an unrelated clause. `maxMinCountByKind` computes,
   per event kind, the largest `min_count` any loaded rule's clause asks
   for; `unfoldEvents` unfolds a group only to the largest of that floor,
   the highest evidenced rank, and one slot per genuine unranked value -
   capped at the group's real historical fold count either way.

## 5. A separate, genuine bug found along the way

Two regenerations of byte-identical input, seconds apart, produced
different `Victims` orderings on the same incident. internal/correlate/
engine.go's `build()` collected victims into a `map[string]bool` and then
ranged over it directly - Go deliberately randomizes map iteration order
per run, so the order was never a decision, just an accident that happened
to look stable in a single-run test. Fixed with `sort.Strings(inc.Victims)`
before returning. This affects the shipped engine, not only replaydemo -
any consumer of `Incident.Victims` was reading a non-deterministic order.

Proven, not just asserted: `TestVictimsAreSortedDeterministically`
(internal/correlate/engine_test.go) fires a `min_count: 5` clause against 5
hosts offered in a scrambled order and checks the result is alphabetical.
Removing the `sort.Strings` call and its import and running the new test 5
times reproduced a different, genuinely random ordering on every run before
the fix was restored.

## 6. Verification

A first attempt compared the two incident arrays with a Node.js script.
This under-reported precision: nanosecond `ts_wall`/`at` values (~1.79e18)
exceed `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` (2^53), so `JSON.parse` silently rounds
them (`1789323631827653632` became `1789323631827653600`), which can hide a
real mismatch or invent a false one. All further comparison used a small
Go program instead, comparing `int64` fields directly with no float64
intermediate, keyed by `(rule_id, root_cause.event_id, root_cause.entity)`
rather than `incident_id` (fresh ULIDs every run are expected and not
compared). Final result, run after every fix above and again after the
last regeneration before this fixture was committed:

    orig count: 16 now count: 16
    TOTAL MISMATCHES: 0

Every field matched except `incident_id` (expected) and `Victims` ordering
(expected - the original file predates the determinism fix above; compared
as sets, not sequences, and the regenerated output independently confirmed
sorted). Every chain link in the regenerated file carries a non-empty
`clause` value; 30 links across 16 incidents, checked directly against
`internal/incident.Link`'s own struct rather than by eyeballing the JSON.

## 7. Regression tests, proven not just asserted

internal/correlate/replaydemo/main_test.go adds unit coverage for
`maxMinCountByKind`, `upsertIncident`, `loadKnownTimestamps`, and
`unfoldEvents` (passthrough, the capped-unfolding fix, the rule's-own-
min_count floor, the redundant-baseline filter, and exact rank placement),
plus one end-to-end regression against the real shipped rules: a folded,
3-count link.down group offered as a single Offer() call must not satisfy
`port-flapping`'s `min_count: 3` (the bug, reproduced), and the same group
run through `unfoldEvents` first must satisfy it (the fix, confirmed).

Each of the two fixes central to this work was deliberately broken and the
suite re-run to confirm it actually fails, not just that it exists:
reverting `unfoldEvents` to a no-op broke the 5 tests that require an
actual split (and left the other 6 passing, as expected of a change that
does not affect a foldCount-1 event); reverting only the capping logic
(`n := foldCount` unconditionally, restoring the exact reachability-lost
class of defect) broke exactly `TestUnfoldEventsDoesNotInventCopiesNothingRequires`.
Both were restored and the full suite re-confirmed green afterward.

## 8. Full regression

    $ go build ./...                               clean
    $ CGO_ENABLED=0 go test ./...                   every package passes
    $ gofmt -l cmd internal                         no output
    $ cd desktop && npm test                        15 files, 130 tests passed

## 9. Live confirmation

desktop/tests/visual/latin-text-scan.spec.ts's KNOWN_GAPS listed the exact
27 English sentences on the Incidents page and 3 on wizard step 5 that fell
back from Arabic before this fix. Run against the regenerated fixture:

    Error: page:incidents: these KNOWN_GAPS entries no longer reproduce - remove them:
    [... all 27 ...]
    Error: wizard-step:5: these KNOWN_GAPS entries no longer reproduce - remove them:
    [... all 3 ...]

Every previously-known gap disappeared and no new violation was reported
on any of the 8 pages or 6 wizard steps scanned - the test's own
"unexpected violations" assertion runs first and stayed silent. KNOWN_GAPS
was emptied accordingly and the suite now passes clean.

## 10. Why this matters beyond one fixture

The defect this work actually found and fixed was in the tooling built
this session to solve an unrelated, smaller problem (a missing i18n key on
old demo data), not in the product. It was caught before demo-incidents.
json was overwritten with unverified output, specifically because the
count did not match what was already known to be correct and that
discrepancy was chased down rather than assumed away. The one real product
fix that came out of it - Victims ordering - was not what anyone went
looking for; it surfaced only because regenerating the same input twice and
diffing the results programmatically is a stronger check than trusting a
single run that merely looks plausible.
